## 21. Special Clinical Situations

### 21.1 Premature ovarian insufficiency and early menopause

Patients with POI generally benefit from systemic estrogen replacement until the average age of natural menopause, unless contraindicated. Treatment may require:

- Higher replacement doses than typical symptom-oriented HT;
- Progestogen when the uterus is present;
- Bone health assessment;
- Cardiovascular risk management;
- Fertility and contraception counseling;
- Evaluation of the cause of ovarian insufficiency;
- Specialist referral or co-management.

Do not manage suspected POI solely through routine menopause telehealth workflows.

### 21.2 Migraine

Menopause-related hormonal fluctuations may worsen migraine. Migraine with aura is not necessarily an absolute contraindication to HT, but avoid unnecessary oral estrogen exposure and consider stable-dose transdermal estradiol. Evaluate new, severe, or neurologically atypical headaches urgently.

### 21.3 Obesity

Obesity increases baseline VTE risk. If systemic HT is appropriate, transdermal estradiol is generally favored, with careful attention to dose, mobility, sleep apnea, blood pressure, and metabolic risk.

### 21.4 Hypertension

Control blood pressure before initiation when possible. Uncontrolled severe hypertension is a contraindication to routine initiation until addressed. Transdermal therapy may be preferred when systemic estrogen is otherwise appropriate.

### 21.5 Diabetes

Diabetes without vascular complications is not an automatic contraindication. Optimize cardiovascular risk factors and consider transdermal therapy when appropriate. Avoid initiating HT in the setting of significant vascular disease without specialist input.

### 21.6 Endometriosis

Systemic estrogen after hysterectomy or ovarian surgery may reactivate residual endometriosis. A progestogen-containing regimen may be considered even without a uterus in selected patients. Refer or co-manage patients with extensive or symptomatic disease.

### 21.7 Fibroids

Fibroids are not an automatic contraindication, but estrogen may contribute to growth or bleeding. Assess symptoms and bleeding pattern; refer when bleeding is significant or diagnosis is uncertain.

